#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# piecewise_expand

#% text
# `piecewise_expand(f)` writes nested piecewise functions, `if`, `max`, `min`, `clip`, `unit_step`, `boole` and `abs` in `f` as one `piecewise([[v1, c1], ...], d)`; `piecewise_expand(f, cond)` also cuts `floor`, `ceiling`, `round` and `prime_pi` of one variable where `cond` bounds it.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# When the conditions are relations the pieces are exclusive: each case's condition is conjoined with the negations of the cases before it, empty pieces are dropped, conditions in one variable that are linear in it are reduced to intervals, and bounds implied by `cond` (or by the assumptions) are left out.

#% item
# A piece with the value 0 becomes the default, otherwise the last case does; the other pieces are sorted by value.

#% item
# When a condition is a symbol the first-match order is kept, each piece with the conditions along its path.

#% item
# `method: ["OrderlessConditions" -> true]` makes exclusive pieces also then; `method: ["BooleanResult" -> true]` gives the disjunction of the pieces' conditions and values; `method: ["ConditionSimplifier" -> reduce]` reduces every condition.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
piecewise_expand(piecewise([[piecewise([[1, x < 1]], 2), x > 0]], 3))
#% output
#= piecewise([[1, 0 < x and x < 1], [2, x >= 1]], 3)

#% cell
piecewise_expand(if x > 0 then max(x, y) else unit_step(y))
#% output
#= piecewise([[1, x <= 0 and y >= 0], [x, x > 0 and x - y >= 0], [y, x > 0 and x - y < 0]], 0)

#% cell
piecewise_expand(max(a, b, c))
#% output
#= piecewise([[a, a - b >= 0 and a - c >= 0], [b, a - b < 0 and b - c >= 0]], c)

#% subsection
# Scope

#% text
# A step function in a bounded interval:

#% cell
piecewise_expand(floor(x), 0 < x and x < 4)
#% output
#= piecewise([[1, 1 <= x and x < 2], [2, 2 <= x and x < 3], [3, x >= 3]], 0)

#% cell
piecewise_expand(prime_pi(x), 0 < x and x < 6)
#% output
#= piecewise([[1, 2 <= x and x < 3], [2, 3 <= x and x < 5], [3, x >= 5]], 0)

#% text
# The interval from an assumption:

#% cell
assuming(0 < x and x < 3, piecewise_expand(floor(x)))
#% output
#= piecewise([[1, 1 <= x and x < 2], [2, x >= 2]], 0)

#% text
# Sums of step functions:

#% cell
piecewise_expand(unit_step(x + 1) - unit_step(x - 1) + 1)
#% output
#= piecewise([[1, x < -1], [2, -1 <= x and x < 1]], 1)

#% text
# Several functions:

#% cell
map(piecewise_expand, [min(x, y), unit_step(x), clip(x, [a, b])])
#% output
#= [piecewise([[x, x - y <= 0]], y), piecewise([[1, x >= 0]], 0), piecewise([[a, a - x > 0], [b, b - x < 0 and a - x <= 0]], x)]

#% subsection
# Options

#% text
# Symbolic conditions keep their order; orderless conditions are exclusive:

#% cell
piecewise_expand(piecewise([[piecewise([[piecewise([[4, a]], 2), b]], 3), c]], 1))
#% output
#= piecewise([[4, c and b and a], [2, c and b], [3, c]], 1)

#% cell
piecewise_expand(piecewise([[piecewise([[piecewise([[4, a]], 2), b]], 3), c]], 1), method: ["OrderlessConditions" -> true])
#% output
#= piecewise([[2, c and b and not a], [3, c and not b], [4, c and b and a]], 1)

#% cell
piecewise_expand(if a then b else c, method: ["BooleanResult" -> true])
#% output
#= a and b or not a and c

#% subsection
# Properties and relations

#% text
# The expansion has the same values:

#% cell
f = max(x, 1 - x, x^2 - 3); g = piecewise_expand(f); [rewrite(f - g, x -> -3), rewrite(f - g, x -> 1/3), rewrite(f - g, x -> 3)]
#% output
#= [0, 0, 0]

#% subsection
# Possible issues

#% text
# Functions that are not piecewise (or not covered) stay as they are, also under a condition:

#% cell
[piecewise_expand(x), piecewise_expand(sign(x)), piecewise_expand(frac(x), 0 < x and x < 2), piecewise_expand(mod(x, 2), 0 < x and x < 5)]
#% output
#= [x, sign(x), frac(x), mod(x, 2)]

#% text
# Without a condition `floor` is not cut (the interval of `x` is unknown):

#% cell
piecewise_expand(floor(x))
#% output
#= floor(x)

#% text
# `round` follows the rounding of the values at the half-integers (to the even integer), so the first piece starts after `x = 1/2`:

#% cell
piecewise_expand(round(x), 0 < x and x < 3)
#% output
#= piecewise([[1, 1/2 < x and x < 3/2], [2, 3/2 <= x and x <= 5/2], [3, x > 5/2]], 0)

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `piecewise`, `max`, `unit_step`, `function_discontinuities`
